  "answer": "I'll add a per-store cart line quantity cap that rejects over-limit updates with HTTP 400 and leaves the cart unchanged. First I'll look at how storefront cart endpoints and merchant configuration work.The cart write paths set a line\u2019s quantity before anything is saved. I\u2019ll reject a quantity above the store\u2019s `CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY` (default 99) before that save, so a 400 leaves the cart unchanged.The quantity check was running after the line was already changed in memory. I\u2019ll check first, then update the line, so a rejected request cannot be flushed.The storefront cart create, add, and update paths now read `CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY` from the store's merchant configuration on each request (default 99) and reject any line that would go above that limit with HTTP 400 before the cart is saved. A quantity of 0 still removes the line.\n\nVERIFICATION: {\"ran\": [\"mvn -pl sm-shop -am test -Dtest=CartLineQuantityLimitTest -DfailIfNoTests=false\"], \"result\": \"passed\", \"could_not_verify\": [\"HTTP 400 from the live cart endpoints and that the persisted cart is unchanged\"], \"missing\": [\"an integration-test fixture that sets CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY and calls POST /api/v1/cart, PUT /api/v1/cart/{code}, and POST /api/v1/cart/{code}/multi\"]}",
